ntdll: commit the ARM64EC code map as it is read, on macOS

The map has one bit for each 4K page of the address space, 4 GB in all, and says which pages
hold ARM64EC code. FEX reads it at every indirect jump and ntdll reads it in RtlIsEcCode, for
any address, so upstream commits all of it read-only and commits the parts that cover loaded
modules writable. Nearly all of it is never looked at.

That is free on Linux, where reading an untouched page gives the shared zero page. macOS has
no such page: a page that is read becomes real memory and counts in the footprint. A program
that reads through its committed memory pays for the whole map. Arknights: Endfield does: with
FEX's lookup tables fixed (patches/fex/0005) it still had 3.6 GB in this map three minutes
after starting.

On macOS the map now starts out reserved. The first read of a page of it faults,
virtual_handle_fault commits that host page read-only (leaving alone the 4K pages already
committed writable) and the read runs again. A reader that asks first what is committed, as a
scanner does, no longer sees the unused parts. The cost is one fault for each 512 MB of
address space a program jumps into.

tests/runtime/scan-memory.c reads every committed page with 16 idle x64 threads alive: 4.5 GB
before (9 GB without the FEX patch), 370 MB after.

--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c	2026-11-18 15:16:34
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c	2026-11-18 15:26:22
@@ -2733,7 +2733,17 @@
     SIZE_T size = ((ULONG_PTR)address_space_limit + page_size) >> (page_shift + 3);  /* one bit per page */
 
     size = ROUND_SIZE( 0, size, host_page_mask );
+#ifdef __APPLE__
+    /* FineWine: the map is 4 GB, one bit for each page of the address space, and almost all of
+     * it is never looked at. Upstream commits all of it read-only, which costs nothing where
+     * reading an untouched page gives the shared zero page. macOS has no such page: every page
+     * that is read becomes real memory, and a program that reads through its committed memory
+     * (anti-cheat does) pays the whole 4 GB. So it starts out reserved, and a page is committed
+     * the first time something reads it, in virtual_handle_fault. */
+    status = map_view( &arm64ec_view, NULL, size, MEM_TOP_DOWN, VPROT_READ, 0, 0, 0 );
+#else
     status = map_view( &arm64ec_view, NULL, size, MEM_TOP_DOWN, VPROT_READ | VPROT_COMMITTED, 0, 0, 0 );
+#endif
     if (status)
     {
         ERR( "failed to allocate ARM64EC map: %08x\n", status );
@@ -4596,6 +4606,25 @@
     }
 #endif
 
+#ifdef __APPLE__
+    /* FineWine: the ARM64EC code map is committed as it is read (see alloc_arm64ec_map). Only
+     * the pages not committed yet are touched: ones that hold code ranges are writable. */
+    if (arm64ec_view && err == EXCEPTION_READ_FAULT &&
+        (char *)addr >= (char *)arm64ec_view->base && (char *)addr < (char *)arm64ec_view->base + arm64ec_view->size &&
+        !(get_page_vprot( addr ) & VPROT_COMMITTED))
+    {
+        char *ptr, *end = min( page + host_page_size, (char *)arm64ec_view->base + arm64ec_view->size );
+
+        for (ptr = page; ptr < end; ptr += page_size)
+            if (!(get_page_vprot( ptr ) & VPROT_COMMITTED))
+                set_page_vprot( ptr, page_size, VPROT_READ | VPROT_COMMITTED );
+        mprotect_range( page, end - page, 0, 0 );
+        mutex_unlock( &virtual_mutex );
+        rec->ExceptionCode = STATUS_SUCCESS;
+        return STATUS_SUCCESS;
+    }
+#endif
+
